cartridge "ROYAL RESCUE/LIGIA LATINO/1990"

' ROYAL RESCUE
' A ColecoVision game based on "Royal Rescue" by Ligia Latino, COMPUTE!'s
' Gazette, January 1990, page 50 (Commodore 64, machine language, SYS
' 2061). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in
' Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: the Dark Castle has 16 rooms in a 4 x 4 grid. Walk, climb
' ladders and jump from ledge to ledge to find the lost royal heir. Keys
' open locked gates (one key each). Treasures score (as shown: cherries 50,
' flowers 100, bells 200 and an extra life up to nine, keys 500, every gate
' opened 1000). Pink marshmallows kill: the roving ones vanish when they
' hit you, the stationary ones stay. A fall of more than about two and a
' half rows, or out of the bottom of a room, kills too. Six lives.
'
' From the original (orig/royal.dis, orig/decode.py; the room maps were
' checked equal to the C64's screen RAM, orig/ramrooms.mjs): the 16 rooms
' with their walls, platforms, belts and ladders; ladders as on the C64: the
' top rung is a floor you can stand on, the rest can only be climbed (you
' stand on the top rung, or at the foot); the objects with their places,
' speeds and bouncing boxes; the score in tens with a fixed 0 printed
' after it; key, bell, gate and treasure rules; walking 2 pixels and
' climbing 1 a C64 frame, the jump (rise about 19 pixels), gravity, the
' fall that kills (landing at 5 pixels a frame or more), walking off a
' ledge drops you straight down, standing over a ladder or its rails does
' not drop you, belts carry you a pixel every third frame, no steering in
' the air, no changing rooms in the air; rooms change by walking off a side
' or climbing off the top (room + 4) or bottom (room - 4); after a death you
' restart where you entered the room.
'
' Liberties: the 40-column rooms are re-laid in 28 columns (rooms.py: each
' ladder placed by its middle column, platforms resampled, the one-column
' gaps beside ladders kept, two hand fixes listed there; compare.mjs checks
' that the hero can stand in the same places as on the C64, room by room);
' the C64's row 0 is dropped for the status line; objects scaled 0.7
' across, speeds converted from 50 to 60 frames a second; the captive is
' "the royal heir" (the C64 says princess); the brazier in the heir's room
' (a sprite that covers the only platform on the way, pixel-collision
' deadly on the C64) is a fire that flares and dies down every 1.7 seconds
' and only burns while it flares; locked gates are tiles that stop you
' (the C64 pushed you back); in the air the hero moves 1.4 pixels a frame
' against 1.17 on foot (the C64: the same speed): the re-laid gaps come out
' a little wider than scaled, and with this the hero can stand in the same
' places as on the C64 (compare.mjs); the platform walkers of rooms 13 and 15 patrol
' their floor (on the C64 they also took ladders at random); keys shown up
' to 9; hit boxes instead of pixel collisions; no play-again question
' (house rule).
'
' Controls: joystick only. Left/right walk, up/down climb, either side
' button jumps. On the title and credits pages a button goes on.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29 and rows 1-22; tiles from code 128;
' the frame loop protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off,
' display off; music first in the sound table, the title tune played once;
' `set sprite count` after every `clear sprites`.

